\title{Universal Finite Libraries:\\Enumeration, Acceptance Probabilities, Catalog Limits, and Distributed Storage}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A universal finite library consists of every word of a fixed length $n$ over an alphabet of size $q$. We prove that it has exactly $q^n$ books and construct a canonical bijection between books and the addresses $0,\ldots,q^n-1$ by base-$q$ expansion. A uniformly sampled complete text consequently has probability exactly $q^{-n}$; for any Boolean acceptance rule, the probability is the number of accepted books divided by $q^n$. We count all book-valued tables on the library, obtaining $(q^n)^{q^n}$, and show that when at least two books exist, no single book can injectively encode every possible such table. This leaves intact concise descriptions of particular structured catalogs. Finally, $T$ distinct records fit into $N$ books of capacity $c$ exactly when $T\le Nc$. The alphabet-four, length-sixteen instance contains $4^{16}=4{,}294{,}967{,}296$ books. Algorithms demonstrate addressing, exact finite acceptance counting, and distributed placement, while a final discussion distinguishes arithmetic enumeration from de Bruijn cycles.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Fix a finite alphabet and a book length, and include every possible word of that length. The result is a finite universal information space. The model applies not only to the Library of Babel, but also to password spaces, bounded program spaces, exhaustive test suites, and fixed-format archives.

Four notions must be separated. \emph{Existence} asks whether a text belongs to the space. \emph{Addressability} asks whether it has a reversible identifier. \emph{Recognizability} asks whether a fixed rule accepts it. \emph{Storage} asks how much arbitrary associated data can be represented. A structured catalog can have a short algorithmic description even though the collection of all arbitrary tables is far too large to be encoded by one book.

We give exact results for all four notions using product counting, positional notation, finite uniform probability, Euclidean division, and the pigeonhole principle.

\section{Finite universal libraries}

\begin{definition}[Alphabet and book]
For $q\ge0$, let $\Sigma_q=\{0,1,\ldots,q-1\}$. A length-$n$ book is a tuple $b=(b_0,\ldots,b_{n-1})$ with every $b_i\in\Sigma_q$. The universal finite library is
\[
\mathcal L(q,n)=\Sigma_q^n.
\]
\end{definition}

The convention includes one empty book when $n=0$. Most applications below have $q\ge2$ and $n\ge1$.

\begin{theorem}[Library Cardinality]
For all finite $q$ and $n$,
\[
|\mathcal L(q,n)|=q^n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ positions admits $q$ independent choices. The product rule gives $q$ multiplied by itself $n$ times. Equivalently, appending one symbol multiplies the number of length-$n$ words by $q$, starting from the unique empty word.
\end{proof}

For $q=25$ and $n=1{,}312{,}000$, the count is $25^{1{,}312{,}000}$. Its decimal representation has
\[
\left\lfloor1{,}312{,}000\log_{10}25\right\rfloor+1
\]
digits, approximately $1.83$ million.

\section{The canonical base-$q$ catalog}
Assume throughout this section that $q\ge2$.

\begin{definition}[Canonical address]
For $b\in\mathcal L(q,n)$, define
\[
\operatorname{addr}(b)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}b_iq^{n-1-i}.
\]
Leading zero symbols are retained, so every book has exactly $n$ digits.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Address range]
Every book satisfies $0\le\operatorname{addr}(b)<q^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The lower bound is immediate. Since $b_i\le q-1$,
\[
\operatorname{addr}(b)\le(q-1)\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}q^j=q^n-1.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{algorithm}[Fixed-length decoding]
Given $a$ with $0\le a<q^n$, initialize $x=a$. For $i=n-1,n-2,\ldots,0$, set $b_i=x\bmod q$ and replace $x$ by $\lfloor x/q\rfloor$. Return $(b_0,\ldots,b_{n-1})$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{lemma}[Mutual inversion]
Decoding $\operatorname{addr}(b)$ returns $b$, and encoding the fixed-length decoding of $a$ returns $a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Reduction modulo $q$ extracts the final digit because all preceding positional terms are divisible by $q$. Division by $q$ then shifts the remaining numeral one place. Repetition recovers every digit. Conversely, each step of Euclidean division satisfies $x=q\lfloor x/q\rfloor+(x\bmod q)$; repeated substitution reconstructs $a$ from the remainders.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Canonical Address Theorem]
The address map is a bijection
\[
\mathcal L(q,n)\longleftrightarrow\{0,1,\ldots,q^n-1\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemma supplies a two-sided inverse, proving injectivity and surjectivity.
\end{proof}

Encoding can be evaluated by Horner's rule, $x_{i+1}=qx_i+b_i$, beginning with $x_0=0$. Encoding and decoding each use $n$ arithmetic operations in a unit-cost model. Bit complexity additionally reflects operations on integers of at most $n\log_2q$ bits.

\begin{corollary}[Four-symbol mini-library]
The library $\mathcal L(4,16)$ contains
\[
4^{16}=2^{32}=4{,}294{,}967{,}296
\]
books and is bijectively cataloged by addresses $0$ through $4{,}294{,}967{,}295$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $q=4$ and $n=16$ into the cardinality and address theorems.
\end{proof}

\section{Uniform probability and recognition}
Give every member of a nonempty library equal probability.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform Text Theorem]
For a fixed $t\in\mathcal L(q,n)$,
\[
\Pr(B=t)=\frac1{q^n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The singleton event contains one of the $q^n$ equally likely outcomes.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Finite Boolean checker]
A checker is a function $C:\mathcal L(q,n)\to\{0,1\}$. Its accepted set and accepted count are
\[
A_C=\{b:C(b)=1\},\qquad M_C=|A_C|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Acceptance Probability]
For a uniformly sampled book $B$,
\[
\Pr(C(B)=1)=\frac{M_C}{q^n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The event is precisely $A_C$. Uniform probability on a finite set is the cardinality of the event divided by the cardinality of the sample space.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Witness Positivity]
If some $w$ satisfies $C(w)=1$, then $M_C\ge1$ and the acceptance probability is positive.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The witness makes $A_C$ nonempty.
\end{proof}

Consequently, a request for the probability of a ``valid proof'' has no unique numerical answer until the alphabet or byte encoding, target statement, grammar, background assumptions, parser, resource policy, and checking rule have been fixed. Once these define a finite Boolean checker, the theorem gives the exact probability. Brute-force calculation of $M_C$ remains exponential in $n$.

\subsection{Patterns}
Let a target pattern have length $k\le n$. At one specified position, exactly $q^{n-k}$ books match, so the probability is $q^{-k}$. For $r=n-k+1$ ordinary starting positions, if $X$ counts occurrences then linearity of expectation gives
\[
\mathbb E[X]=rq^{-k}.
\]
The union bound gives
\[
\Pr(X\ge1)\le rq^{-k}.
\]
Thus ``opportunities times $q^{-k}$'' is an exact expected count and an upper bound, but not generally an exact occurrence probability: overlapping pattern events can be dependent.

\section{All complete catalog tables}
Let $L=q^n$.

\begin{definition}[Complete book-valued table]
A complete table is any function $f:\mathcal L(q,n)\to\mathcal L(q,n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Table Count]
There are exactly
\[
L^L=(q^n)^{q^n}
\]
complete book-valued tables.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At each of $L$ inputs, the table independently chooses one of $L$ outputs. The product rule gives $L^L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Single-Book Universal Table Impossibility]
If $L\ge2$, no injective encoding assigns every complete book-valued table to one book in the library.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The proposed encoding would map a set of size $L^L$ to one of size $L$. Since $L^L\ge L^2>L$ for $L\ge2$, the pigeonhole principle forces a collision.
\end{proof}

This theorem concerns every possible table, not one particular structured catalog. The canonical address map is one regular function and has a concise algorithmic description. There is no conflict between that fact and the impossibility of representing all arbitrary tables injectively in the original book format.

Information counting gives the same comparison: distinguishing all tables requires at least
\[
\log_2(L^L)=L\log_2L
\]
bits, whereas distinguishing the books requires $\log_2L$ bits. Physical capacity claims additionally require a specified symbol encoding.

\section{Distributed storage}
Suppose $T$ distinct records are to occupy $N$ books, each containing $c$ distinguishable slots.

\begin{theorem}[Distributed Capacity Theorem]
An injective placement exists if and only if
\[
T\le Nc.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Necessity follows because there are only $Nc$ book-slot pairs. For sufficiency, when $c>0$, number records $0,\ldots,T-1$ and send record $i$ to
\[
\left(\left\lfloor\frac{i}{c}\right\rfloor, i\bmod c\right).
\]
Euclidean division makes the pairs distinct. Since $i<T\le Nc$, the book index is below $N$, and the remainder is below $c$. If $c=0$, the inequality forces $T=0$, and the empty placement works.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Minimum book count]
If $c>0$, the least number of books required for $T$ records is
\[
\left\lceil\frac{T}{c}\right\rceil.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
This is the least integer $N$ satisfying $T\le Nc$.
\end{proof}

Materializing all locations takes $O(T)$ arithmetic operations and $O(T)$ output space. A single location can be computed on demand with one quotient-remainder operation. For example, $23$ records at capacity $5$ require $\lceil23/5\rceil=5$ books.

\section{Algorithms and applications}
The address bijection supports exhaustive testing by partitioning the interval $[0,q^n)$ among workers. Each worker decodes its addresses and evaluates a checker. Exhaustive traversal takes $O(q^n(n+\tau_C))$ time when a checker evaluation costs $\tau_C$, while streaming uses only $O(n)$ working storage.

For binary words of length eight, consider the checker accepting exactly four ones. It accepts
\[
\binom84=70
\]
books, and hence has probability $70/256$. This illustrates how a semantic rule becomes a cardinality problem once made finite and explicit.

The same framework describes uniform password spaces and bounded test inputs. A single fixed secret has probability $q^{-n}$ per independent uniform guess; a policy accepting $M$ candidates occupies fraction $M/q^n$ of the space. Real human choices are not uniform, so these values are baselines rather than behavioral models.

The distributed placement rule is elementary sharding: quotient selects a shard and remainder selects a local slot. More realistic systems add variable lengths, replication, failures, and update constraints, but cannot evade the foundational capacity inequality.

\section{De Bruijn cycles}
\begin{definition}[de Bruijn cycle]
A de Bruijn cycle of order $n$ over $\Sigma_q$ is a cyclic word of length $q^n$ in which every member of $\mathcal L(q,n)$ appears exactly once as a cyclic window of length $n$.
\end{definition}

A de Bruijn cycle is stronger than the arithmetic catalog: it arranges all words so consecutive windows overlap in $n-1$ symbols. For $q=4$ and $n=16$, its cyclic length would be $4^{16}$.

A standard route uses a directed graph with length-$15$ words as vertices and length-$16$ words as edges. An edge runs from its first $15$ symbols to its last $15$ symbols. Every vertex has indegree and outdegree $4$. After establishing connectivity, an Eulerian circuit traverses every edge exactly once, and its labels yield the cycle. Completing this development requires explicit cyclic-window definitions, balanced-degree and connectivity proofs, an Eulerian-circuit theorem, and a unique-window argument. None of the preceding results assumes that construction.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The results expose three boundaries. Existence is not discoverability: every fixed target occurs, but random discovery has probability $q^{-n}$. A structured rule is not arbitrary data: positional notation gives one concise catalog, while all tables number $L^L$. Finally, symbol count is not semantics: an exact acceptance probability requires a fully specified checker.

Future work should construct de Bruijn cycles explicitly, specialize them to the four-symbol order-sixteen library, and compare overlap traversal with arithmetic enumeration. A second direction is a concrete finite tokenization and bounded proof-like language. Its encoding, target, parser, inference environment, and resource limits must all be fixed; the acceptance formula remains $M_C/q^n$, while the challenge becomes calculating or bounding $M_C$. Other extensions include nonuniform distributions, automata-based pattern counting, variable-length books, compression, replicated storage, and erasure tolerance.

\section{Conclusion}
A length-$n$ universal library over $q$ symbols has exactly $q^n$ books and a canonical reversible base-$q$ address system. Uniform sampling assigns probability $q^{-n}$ to one specified text and $M_C/q^n$ to a checker accepting $M_C$ books. All complete book-valued tables number $(q^n)^{q^n}$, preventing injective single-book representation of every table when at least two books exist. Distributed storage has the exact constructive criterion $T\le Nc$. These results give a self-contained mathematics of finite universal information: complete in content, canonical in address, measurable under explicit recognition, limited in arbitrary self-description, and expandable through distribution.

\end{document}

